  "opennyai_summary": {
    "PREAMBLE": "Sh. Rajesh Batra vs The State (Delhi Administration) on 14 May,\n2016\n 1\n In The Court Of Sh. Rakesh Pandit,\n Asj-01, New Delhi District, Patiala House Courts, New Delhi\nCa No. 09/16\nSh. Rajesh Batra\nS/o Ishwar Das Batra\nR/o 520, Jheel Kurenja,\nDelhi-110051.\n..\n Vs.\nThe State (Delhi Administration) .... \n Date of receiving of Appeal : 18.01.2016\n Date of arguments : 11.05.2016\n Date of judgment : 14.05.2016\n Judgment\n",
    "facts": "3 As the sample was failed, prosecution was launched by Food Inspector by filing complaint dated 08.09.2004 with the court.\nHe has exercised his right u/sec.13(2) PFA Act to get the sample analyzed by CFL.\nCharge was framed against accused u/sec.2(ia) (a) (j) (m) read with sec.7 of PFA Act, 1954 punishable u/sec.16(1A) of PFA Act.\n",
    "issue": "( i) The commodity i.e. Dal Arhar was not for sale but was for private consumption by the employees of the accused.\n",
    "ANALYSIS": "Similarly, in Shashi Kant Vs. State of UP, 1983 (1)\nPrevention of Food Adulteration Cases 90, it was observed that the \"bhagona\" was not proved to be clean and dry.\nCA No. 09/16 Rajesh Batra Vs. State  14\nSo, accordingly, it can be said that the law which can be crystallized from various judgments of Hon. High Courts as well as Hon. Supreme Court is that if in evidence, the prosecution states that the sample commodity had touched a surface of an \"implement\" or any other surface, it is the duty of the prosecution to prove beyond reasonable doubt that the said implement/surface was clean and dry and thus could not have added any impurity to the sample.\nHowever, it is not the law that the FI shall spell out on which surfaces the sample commodity had touched while sampling.\nHowever, if he chooses to spell out the implements/surfaces with which the sample commodity came into contact/touch, the same must be proved as clean and dry.\nSh. Rajesh Batra vs The State (Delhi Administration) on 14 May, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/120084627/\n3 15\nIn the present case, neither PW1 nor PW2 had stated anything about as to how the Dal was weighed.\nThe electronic balance was kept by the vendor for weighing the food articles like Dal, spices etc.\nNow no where prosecution had deposed that this electronic balance was clean and dry and free from any colour.\nThus there is violation of Rule 14 of PFA Rules.\n16 So, in these circumstances, the prosecution failed to prove the strict compliance u/sec.14 of PFA Rules.\n",
    "decision": "17 So, the judgment and order on sentence of the trial court is reversed.\n18 Accused Rajesh Batra is acquitted of the charges u/sec.16(1A) read with sec.7 of PFA Act.\nAccept months.\nVide separate judgment the appeal filed by the appellant is disposed is allowed.\n"
  },
